CAT = "iepsaPowers"; SEG = "POW"
# Criminal Law Act 1997 ss. 2 and 4 (arrest without warrant by any person); Non-Fatal Offences
# Against the Person Act 1997 ss. 18 and 20 (justifiable force); Criminal Justice (Public Order) Act
# 1994 ss. 11 and 13 (trespass); Criminal Justice (Theft and Fraud Offences) Act 2001 s. 4 (theft,
# up to 10 years on indictment); QQI 4N20604 learning outcomes 2.1, 2.5, 2.6, 2.7.
Q = [
("Under which law does a security guard in Ireland arrest someone without a warrant?",
 "Section 4, Criminal Law Act 1997", ["Section 29, Private Security Act", "Section 13, Public Order Act", "Section 18, Non-Fatal Offences Act"],
 "Section 4 of the Criminal Law Act 1997 gives any person a power of arrest without warrant for an arrestable offence. The Private Security Services Act gives guards no extra arrest power."),
("What is an arrestable offence under the Criminal Law Act 1997?",
 "One with 5+ years' jail", ["Any offence at all", "One punishable by a fine only", "Any offence seen on CCTV"],
 "Section 2 defines an arrestable offence as one for which a person of full capacity, not previously convicted, may be punished by imprisonment for five years or more, and it includes an attempt."),
("Is theft an arrestable offence?",
 "Yes, up to 10 years' jail", ["No, it is a minor offence", "Only above a certain value", "Only on a second offence"],
 "Section 4(6) of the Criminal Justice (Theft and Fraud Offences) Act 2001 sets a maximum of 10 years' imprisonment for theft, above the five years that makes an offence arrestable."),
("Is an attempt to commit an arrestable offence itself arrestable?",
 "Yes, it is included", ["No, only completed offences", "Only if property is taken", "Only with a Garda present"],
 "Section 2 of the Criminal Law Act 1997 says an arrestable offence includes an attempt to commit any such offence."),
("Under section 4(1), a guard may arrest someone who is, or who he reasonably suspects to be:",
 "Committing an arrestable offence", ["Planning a crime for later this week", "Known to the Gardaí already", "Behaving strangely on site"],
 "Section 4(1) lets any person arrest without warrant anyone who is, or whom he or she with reasonable cause suspects to be, in the act of committing an arrestable offence."),
("A stock room was broken into last night. Under section 4(2), when may a guard arrest a suspect today?",
 "If it was really committed", ["Whenever he has a hunch", "Only on the same night", "Only with the owner's consent"],
 "Section 4(2) applies only where an arrestable offence has been committed. If no offence was committed, a private person's arrest on suspicion is not covered, unlike a Garda's under section 4(3)."),
("Besides the offence itself, what extra condition does section 4(4) set for a guard's arrest?",
 "He'd otherwise evade the Gardaí", ["The client has agreed to it in writing", "Two guards are present", "The suspect is over 18"],
 "Section 4(4) says a person other than a Garda may arrest only where he or she, with reasonable cause, suspects the person would otherwise attempt to avoid, or is avoiding, arrest by a Garda."),
("After a guard arrests someone, what does section 4(5) require?",
 "Hand them to the Gardaí ASAP", ["Hold them until the shift ends", "Take them to the manager", "Release them after a warning"],
 "Section 4(5) says a person arrested by anyone other than a Garda shall be transferred into Garda custody as soon as practicable."),
("A shoplifter gives his name and address and is clearly not trying to get away. A guard arrests him anyway. What is the problem?",
 "No sign he'd evade the Gardaí", ["Shoplifting is not theft", "Guards may only arrest at night", "The name must be checked first"],
 "Under section 4(4) a private person may arrest only where there is reasonable cause to suspect the person would otherwise avoid arrest by a Garda. The guard can report the theft to the Gardaí."),
("Which phrase best describes the force a guard may use in making a lawful arrest?",
 "Only what is reasonable", ["Whatever stops the person", "None, ever", "As much as the client allows"],
 "Section 18 of the Non-Fatal Offences Against the Person Act 1997 protects the use of force only if it is such as is reasonable in the circumstances as the person believes them to be."),
("For which purpose does section 18 of the 1997 Act allow reasonable force?",
 "To prevent a crime", ["To punish a thief", "To get an apology", "To recover an unpaid debt"],
 "Section 18(1)(e) allows reasonable force to prevent crime or a breach of the peace. Force used as punishment or revenge is never justified."),
("Under section 20 of the 1997 Act, holding a person against their will without striking them counts as:",
 "Using force", ["No force at all", "A citizen's warning", "A legal search"],
 "Section 20(1)(b) says a person is treated as using force in relation to another if he or she detains that person, even without actually using force."),
("Under section 20, is threatening force treated the same as using it?",
 "Yes, a threat counts as force", ["No, threats are always lawful", "Only if written down", "Only if a weapon is shown"],
 "Section 20(1)(b)(i) treats a threat to use force as a use of force, so a threat must also be reasonable. Section 20(3) adds that a threat may be reasonable where actual force would not be."),
("When a court judges whether a guard's force was reasonable, what does section 20(4) say it will consider?",
 "Whether he could retreat", ["His years of service", "The value of the goods", "The client's instructions"],
 "Section 20(4) says the fact that a person had an opportunity to retreat before using force is taken into account with other evidence in deciding whether the force was reasonable."),
("A thief has dropped the stolen goods and is walking away. A guard punches him. Why is this unlawful?",
 "The force is no longer needed", ["Guards may never touch anyone", "The goods were recovered", "He did not warn first"],
 "Force is lawful only while it is reasonable for a protective purpose. Once the threat has passed, a blow is retaliation and can be an assault."),
("A Garda is arresting a man and a guard, misreading the scene, pushes the Garda away. What does section 18(6) say?",
 "No defence: Garda on duty", ["It is fine if he meant well", "It is fine on private land", "Only the Garda is at fault"],
 "Section 18(6) removes the defence where the person knows force is used against a Garda acting in the course of duty, unless it is believed immediately necessary to prevent harm to himself or another."),
("What does it mean to 'dishonestly appropriate' property under the 2001 Theft Act?",
 "Take it as yours, no consent", ["Buy it at a low price", "Borrow it with the owner's permission", "Find it and hand it in"],
 "Section 4(1) says a person is guilty of theft if he or she dishonestly appropriates property without the owner's consent, intending to deprive the owner of it."),
("A man gives a false name to borrow a pallet truck and never returns it. Is consent obtained this way valid?",
 "No, deception voids it", ["Yes, the owner agreed", "Only if he paid a deposit", "Yes, if under one day"],
 "Section 4(2) of the 2001 Act says consent obtained by deception or intimidation is not consent for theft purposes."),
("What is a trespasser in the security sense?",
 "Someone there without permission", ["Any visitor after 6 pm", "Anyone without photo ID", "A contractor working on site after hours"],
 "A trespasser is a person on land or in a building without the owner's or occupier's permission, or who stays after it is withdrawn. Being there without permission is not, on its own, one of the 1994 Act offences: section 11 needs intent to commit an offence and section 13 conduct likely to cause fear."),
("A man found inside a closed warehouse at night with bolt cutters may have committed which offence?",
 "Trespassing to commit an offence", ["Being drunk and disorderly in a public place", "Failing to produce photo ID", "Loitering near a public road"],
 "Section 11 of the Criminal Justice (Public Order) Act 1994 makes it an offence to enter a building as a trespasser where the circumstances suggest intent to commit an offence or interfere with property."),
("A trespasser in a car park is causing no harm. What is the correct first step?",
 "Ask him to leave politely", ["Arrest him immediately", "Search his car for evidence", "Take his car keys away"],
 "Being there without permission, with no intent to commit an offence and no conduct causing fear, is not a 1994 Act offence. The guard asks the person to leave, explains why, and only then considers calling the Gardaí."),
("Who may direct a person trespassing in a way that causes fear to leave the vicinity under section 13 of the 1994 Act?",
 "A Garda", ["Any licensed guard", "The site cleaner", "A PSA inspector"],
 "Section 13(2) gives a Garda the power to direct a person suspected of trespassing in a way likely to cause fear to stop and leave. Failing to obey that direction is an offence."),
("A guard wants to look in a staff member's locker. When may he lawfully search it?",
 "With consent or by contract", ["Whenever he suspects theft", "Only on a Friday", "Whenever the locker is shut"],
 "A guard has no general power of search. A search of a person's property needs their consent or a lawful basis such as an agreed condition of employment or entry."),
("A shopper refuses a bag search requested at the exit. What should the guard do?",
 "Accept it and report it", ["Take the bag by force", "Lock her in the shop", "Search it when she looks away"],
 "A bag search is by consent. Without an arrestable offence and the section 4 conditions, the guard records the refusal and informs the supervisor or Gardaí if there is real suspicion."),
("Where a personal search is agreed, who should carry it out?",
 "A same-sex guard", ["Any guard on duty", "Only the supervisor", "The person's friend"],
 "Good practice, and most site procedures, require a same-sex searcher with a witness present, to protect the person's dignity and the guard against false allegations."),
("During a consented bag search, why should a guard ask the person to open the bag and move items?",
 "Avoids injury and claims", ["It is faster that way", "Guards may not touch bags", "It saves on gloves"],
 "Letting the person handle their own items cuts the risk of needle-stick or sharp injuries and of claims that the guard damaged or planted something."),
("A man starts punching a shop assistant. May a guard use reasonable force to protect her?",
 "Yes, to protect another", ["No, only to protect himself", "Only if she is his family", "Only once the Gardaí arrive"],
 "Section 18(1)(a) of the 1997 Act covers reasonable force to protect yourself, a family member or another person from injury, assault or detention caused by a criminal act."),
("While waiting for the Gardaí after an arrest, the suspect says he feels unwell and has chest pain. What do you do?",
 "Get medical help at once", ["Ignore it as a trick", "Let him go home", "Make him sign a statement"],
 "A guard who detains someone is responsible for their welfare. Chest pain needs emergency help; tell the Gardaí and record what happened."),
("A guard arrests a teenager for theft but has no reasonable cause, and holds him in a room for an hour. What might the guard face?",
 "False imprisonment", ["A promotion at work", "A formal thank-you letter", "Nothing, the boy is under 18"],
 "Detaining someone without lawful authority is false imprisonment: an offence under section 15 of the Non-Fatal Offences Against the Person Act 1997 and a civil wrong for which the guard and employer can be sued."),
("After an arrest, the client tells the guard to take the suspect's phone to check his photos. Should he?",
 "No, that is for the Gardaí", ["Yes, to gather evidence", "Yes, with the client's consent", "Only if it is unlocked"],
 "A guard has no power to search a suspect's phone. Evidence gathering after arrest is for the Gardaí, who have the legal powers and procedures."),
("A guard sees a man smashing a car window in the car park. Can he arrest him?",
 "Yes, if he'd evade the Gardaí", ["No, cars are not property", "Only if he owns the car", "Only after sending a warning letter"],
 "Criminal damage under section 2 of the Criminal Damage Act 1991 can carry up to 10 years' imprisonment on indictment, so it is arrestable. The section 4(4) condition still applies: reasonable cause to suspect he would otherwise avoid Garda arrest."),
("Which is the safest way to deal with a suspect who runs off after a theft?",
 "Note details, call the Gardaí", ["Chase him off the site", "Throw something at him", "Follow him in your own car to his home"],
 "Chasing a suspect off site puts the guard and the public at risk. A good description, direction of travel and CCTV help the Gardaí more than a risky pursuit."),
]
